  "text": "Allergies:\nA.C.E Inhibitors / Morphine Sulfate / Cimetidine / Lovastatin\n\nChief Complaint:\nFatigue/DOE; worsening renal failure\n\nHistory of Present Illness:\nDr. ___ is an ___ yo male with a PMH significant for CRI, AAA \nrepair, CAD, s/p splenectomy, who presents with dysnpea and \nweakness upon exertion. He is a very pleasant retired BI \nintern___ who is knowledgable about his current health issues \nand PMH. \n\nOver the last few months, he has become increasingly dyspnic and \nweak upon exertion. He attributed these symptoms to his chronic \nrenal insufficiency (secondary to longstanding HTN) for which he \nhas been followed by Dr. ___ for over ___ years.    \n\n\nThis past week, the patient's symptoms continued to progress \nwhere he could ___ longer walk more than 10 steps without fatigue \nand SOB. He also noticed increased swelling in his right leg. \nThe patient called Dr. ___ concerning these \nsymptoms. They had a lengthy discussion concerning CRF \ntherapeutic options and end-of-life care. The patient decided to \nprocede with dialysis and was scheduled for an outpatient \nevaluation ___ however, his symptoms continued to \nprogress. He was admitted to the ___ medicine floor ___ \n___ for inpatient dialysis and further medical monitoring of \nsymptoms. \n\nAt the time of admission, initial vitals were T=98.2, BP= \n110/70, HR=52, RR=18, O2sat=100%. He reports SOB & weakness \nafter walking several steps. He notes increased swelling in the \nright leg, concentrated around the ankle. He denies any recent \nsyncopal eposides or falls. \n\nReview of sytems:  \n(+) Per HPI  \n(-) Denies fever, chills, night sweats, recent weight loss or \ngain. Denies headache, sinus tenderness, rhinorrhea or \ncongestion. Denied cough, chest pain or tightness, or \npalpitations. Denied nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, constipation or \nabdominal pain. ___ recent change in bowel habits (5 black stools \nper day) or urination. ___ dysuria. Denied arthralgias or \nmyalgias.\n\nBrief Hospital Course:\nDr. ___ is an ___ yo male with a PMH significant for CRI, AAA \nrepair, CAD, s/p splenectomy, who presents with dysnpea and \nweakness upon exertion and worsening renal failure approximately \none month before planned initiation of HD as an outpatient.  \n\n# ESRD/initiation of hemodialysis: Dr. ___ was evaluated by \nthe renal consult team at the time of admission and the decision \nwas made to admit the patient for initiation of dialysis as an \ninpatient.  He had a tunneled line placed on ___ followed by \nthree sessions of inpatient hemodialysis on consecutive days. \nThese sessions resulted in improvement of mild acidosis and \nelecctrolyte disturbances. The patient wished to be discharged \nprior to final arrangements for outpatient HD placement. \nBaseline ECG, hepatitis serologies (negative) and PTH were \notained. Given his history (per self-report) of \nstrongly-positive PPD, a Quantiferon Gold study was sent on \n___ to be followed as an outpatient. He may require treatment \nwith INH if result returns positive. CXR during admission was \nclear. He will follow up on ___ to \ninitiate outpatient HD. Prior to discharge, he also underwent \nvein mapping to facilitate eventual fistula placement. Weakness \nand fatigue improved after initiation of HD and transfusions as \nbelow.\n\n# Anemia: The patient was noted to have a 4-point Hct drop from \n25 five days prior to admission to 21 on arrival. He reported \n___ black stools per day, but stated that these bowel patterns \nwere stable for several years and that dark stool color was \nattributed to iron supplementation. He repoted guaiac tests \napproximately every 6 months in the past which had always been \nnegative. Iron studies were sent and returned low-normal. \nHowever, the morning following admission patient's Hct fell \nfurther to 18, and guaiac test at that time was strongly \nheme-positive. He received 3 units of packed RBCs, with \nappropriate response to Hct > 25. This remained stable over the \nremainder of the admission. GI work up (upper/lower endoscopy) \nwas recommended to patient to identify source of bleed, but at \nthe time of discharge he maintained that he was not interested. \nHe will follow up further on this issue with his PCP. This \nsudden drop in Hct likely contributed to patient's \nweakness/fatigue as well as his rapidly-declining renal \nfunction. He will receive erythropoeitin in conjunction with HD. \nAspirin, iron were held at discharge.\n\n# Bilateral pedal edema: Most likely secondary to CRI. Improved \nslightly with initiation of HD. Lasix was held at discharge.\n\n# HTN: Home dose of atenolol was reduced to 25 mg and may \nrequire further titration as an outpatient (or change to \nmetoprolol, as not renally metabolized). \n\n# Macular degeneration: Icaps were not available on formulary \nand were therefore held during this admission.\n\n# CAD: Atenolol as above. Holding isosorbide dinitrate for now \nas patient on special formulation which may not be available as \ninpatient. \n\n# Insomnia: 2.5 mg PO valium 1 MR1 for sleep while inpatient.\n\nACCESS: PIV's, tunneled HD line\nPROPHYLAXIS: DVT ppx with penumoboots\nCODE: DNR, DNI  \nContact: HCP is son ___ ___\n \n___ on Admission:\nATENOLOL - 50 mg Tablet - 1 Tablet(s) by mouth daily\nCALCITRIOL - 0.25 mg once daily  \nDOXAZOSIN - 4 mg - 1 Tablet(s) by mouth twice daily\nERYTHROPOIETIN - 12,000 units every week \nFLUTICASONE - 50 mcg Spray ___ach nare qhs\nFUROSEMIDE - 80g\nISOSORBIDE DINITRATE - daily in morning \nASPIRIN - 325 mg once daily \nFERROUS SULFATE - 325 mg daily \nICaps - once daily \nMULTIVITAMIN\n\nDischarge Diagnosis:\nPrimary: \n- End-stage renal disease with initiation of hemodialysis\n- Anemia/falling hematocrit with guaiac-positive stool\n\nSecondary:\n- Hypertension\n- Gout",
